   "source": [
    "**Conclusion**\n",
    "\n",
    "The CEDS dataset is a robust and comprehensive source of greenhouse gas emissions data, making it highly suitable for our analysis of the relationship between emissions and weather patterns. Its extensive temporal and geographical coverage, combined with its scientific credibility, allows us to draw meaningful insights into how different gases have contributed to climate change over time and across regions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "0-v7dAqcJABn"
   },
   "source": [
    "**Key Points for Data Preparation**:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Ensure number of data points per country is sufficient for analysis.\n",
    "2. Handle missing values for key gases (e.g., CH4, N2O, CO2) appropriately, either through imputation or exclusion.\n",
    "3. Consider normalising emissions data to account for differences in country size and economic activity when correlating with weather patterns.\n",
    "4. Align temporal coverage with weather data to ensure meaningful correlation analysis.\n",
    "5. For country-level analysis, choosing to use fuel-level or sector-level is crucial, since we don't have contry-level data with both fuel and sector."
   ]
  },
