   "source": [
    "Descriptive Analysis: Summary Statistics\n",
    "\n",
    "To establish a numerical baseline for our country-level analysis, we calculated the standard summary statistics (mean, median, standard deviation, and range) for both our target variable (Average Temperature) and our independent variables (Greenhouse Gas Emissions).\n",
    "\n",
    "1. The Target Variable: Average Temperature Baseline\n",
    "- Central Tendency: The global historical average temperature across all recorded country-years is 18.424160 °C, with a median (50th percentile) of 20.164545°C.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Dispersion and Extremes: The data captures a massive global climate range, spanning from a minimum of -5.113667°C to a maximum of 31.082625°C.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Volatility: The standard deviation of 7.636591°C indicates the average spread of temperatures across different regions and years, confirming significant geographical variation in our dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "2. The Independent Variables: Emission Distributions and Skewness\n",
    "\n",
    "A review of the 10 greenhouse gas (GHG) and pollutant variables reveals a highly skewed distribution across the globe, driven by extreme industrial outliers.Massive Positive Skew: For nearly all gases, the mean is significantly higher than the median (50th percentile). For example, the mean CO2 emission is 5.442175e+04, while the median is only 4.206583e+02. \n",
    "\n",
    "This mathematical gap proves that the \"average\" is being dragged upward by a small handful of massive polluters, rather than indicating that every country emits a high volume.The Outlier Effect: The maximum values (max) for gases like $CO_2$, $CH_4$, and $SO_2$ are exponentially larger than their 75th percentiles. This confirms that a vast majority of global emissions are concentrated in the absolute top tier of industrialized nations (the 99th percentile), underscoring the inequality of the global pollution footprint.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Data Completeness\n",
    "\n",
    "The count for all variables is 25591, confirming that our dataset is robust and there are no critical missing values (NaNs) across the country-year intersections that would skew our upcoming diagnostic models"
   ]
