  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### LSTM "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory) is a type of recurrent neural network (RNN) that is well-suited for modeling sequential data, such as time series. LSTMs are designed to capture long-term dependencies in the data, which can be particularly important when analyzing climate data that may have complex temporal patterns."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "df = ds_country.copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Cumulate emissions by country\n",
    "for gas in gas_cols:\n",
    "    df[f\"{gas}_cumulative\"] = df.groupby(\"country\")[gas].cumsum()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We process the data to create sequences of input features and corresponding target values for training the LSTM model:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Loop through each country in the dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "- Scale the emissions data for each country using Min-Max Scaling to ensure that the features are on a similar scale, which can help improve the performance of the model.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Create sequences of x years of emissions data as input features, and the corresponding average temperature of the next year as the target variable.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Split the sequences into training and testing sets based on a time-based split, ensuring that the model is trained on historical data and tested on more recent data."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "window_size = 5  # number of past years to use\n",
    "\n",
    "# Function to create sequences for time-series modeling\n",
    "def create_sequences(data, window_size=5, target_index=2):\n",
    "    X, y = [], []\n",
    "    # Loop through the data to create sequences of features and corresponding targets\n",
    "    for i in range(len(data) - window_size):\n",
    "        X.append(data[i:i+window_size])\n",
    "        y.append(data[i+window_size, target_index])\n",
    "    return np.array(X), np.array(y)\n",
    "\n",
    "X_list_train, X_list_test = [], []\n",
    "y_list_train, y_list_test = [], []\n",
    "country_test = []\n",
    "year_test_list = []\n",
    "scalers = {}\n",
    "\n",
    "# Loop through each country, scale the features, create sequences, and split into train/test sets\n",
    "for country in df['country'].unique():\n",
    "    df_country = df[df['country'] == country].sort_values('year')\n",
    "    df_numeric = df_country.drop(columns=['country','ISO3','continent']).reset_index(drop=True)\n",
    "    target_index = df_numeric.columns.get_loc('AvgTemp')\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Min-Max scale using only the training portion to prevent data leakage\n",
    "    scaling_split = int(len(df_numeric) * 0.8)\n",
    "    scaler = MinMaxScaler()\n",
    "    scaler.fit(df_numeric.iloc[:scaling_split])\n",
    "    scaled = scaler.transform(df_numeric)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Create sequences for this country\n",
    "    X_country, y_country = create_sequences(scaled, window_size, target_index)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # 80/20 split for train/test sets\n",
    "    split_seq = int(len(X_country) * 0.8)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    X_list_train.append(X_country[:split_seq])\n",
    "    y_list_train.append(y_country[:split_seq])\n",
    "    \n",
    "    X_list_test.append(X_country[split_seq:])\n",
    "    y_list_test.append(y_country[split_seq:])\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Keep track of which country and years are in the test set for later analysis\n",
    "    \n",
    "    country_test.extend([country] * len(y_country[split_seq:]))\n",
    "\n",
    "    years = df_country['year'].values\n",
    "    seq_years = years[window_size:]  # align with y\n",
    "    years_test = seq_years[split_seq:]\n",
    "    year_test_list.extend(years_test)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    scalers[country] = scaler  # Save scaler to inverse transform later"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We convert all data into PyTorch tensors and create DataLoader objects for efficient batching during training."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Combine all countries' train and test data into single tensors for modeling\n",
    "X_train = torch.tensor(np.concatenate(X_list_train), dtype=torch.float32)\n",
    "y_train = torch.tensor(np.concatenate(y_list_train), dtype=torch.float32)\n",
    "\n",
    "X_test = torch.tensor(np.concatenate(X_list_test), dtype=torch.float32)\n",
    "y_test = torch.tensor(np.concatenate(y_list_test), dtype=torch.float32)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Create DataLoaders for batch processing during model training\n",
    "train_loader = DataLoader(TensorDataset(X_train, y_train), batch_size=128, shuffle=True)\n",
    "test_loader = DataLoader(TensorDataset(X_test, y_test), batch_size=128, shuffle=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "year_test_all = np.array(year_test_list)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We build a simple LSTM model using PyTorch, which consists of an LSTM layer followed by a fully connected layer to output the predicted temperature."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Define the LSTM model\n",
    "class LSTMRegressor(nn.Module):\n",
    "    def __init__(self, input_size, hidden_size=64, num_layers=1, dropout=0.1, bidirectional=False):\n",
    "        super(LSTMRegressor, self).__init__()\n",
    "        \n",
    "        self.lstm = nn.LSTM(\n",
    "            input_size, hidden_size,\n",
    "            num_layers=num_layers,\n",
    "            batch_first=True,\n",
    "            bidirectional=bidirectional\n",
    "        )\n",
    "        \n",
    "        self.dropout = nn.Dropout(dropout) # Regularization to prevent overfitting\n",
    "        self.out = nn.Linear(hidden_size, 1)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def forward(self, x):\n",
    "        out, _ = self.lstm(x)\n",
    "        out = out[:, -1, :]      # last timestep\n",
    "        out = self.dropout(out)\n",
    "        out = self.out(out)\n",
    "        return out\n",
    "\n",
    "input_size = X_train.shape[2]\n",
    "model = LSTMRegressor(input_size)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Move model to GPU if available\n",
    "device = torch.device(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\")\n",
    "model.to(device)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We use:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss function to measure the difference between the predicted and actual temperatures.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Adam optimizer to update the model weights during training.\n",
    "\n",
    "- ReduceLROnPlateau learning rate scheduler to reduce the learning rate if the validation loss does not improve for a certain number of epochs."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "criterion = nn.MSELoss()\n",
    "optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=0.001)\n",
    "scheduler = torch.optim.lr_scheduler.ReduceLROnPlateau(optimizer, 'min', patience=10, factor=0.5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Training can be time-consuming, especially with a large dataset and complex model architecture. You can expect 5-10 minutes running this in a normal Google Colab environment."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "epochs = 100\n",
    "best_loss = float('inf')\n",
    "patience = 30\n",
    "counter = 0\n",
    "\n",
    "# Training loop \n",
    "for epoch in range(epochs):\n",
    "    model.train()\n",
    "    train_loss = 0\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Loop through batches of training data\n",
    "    for X_batch, y_batch in train_loader:\n",
    "        # Calculate loss and backpropagate\n",
    "        optimizer.zero_grad()\n",
    "        output = model(X_batch).squeeze()\n",
    "        loss = criterion(output, y_batch)\n",
    "        loss.backward()\n",
    "        optimizer.step()\n",
    "        train_loss += loss.item()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    train_loss /= len(train_loader)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Validation\n",
    "    model.eval()\n",
    "    val_loss = 0\n",
    "    with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "        # Loop through batches of test data to calculate validation loss\n",
    "        for X_batch, y_batch in test_loader:\n",
    "            output = model(X_batch).squeeze()\n",
    "            loss = criterion(output, y_batch)\n",
    "            val_loss += loss.item()\n",
    "    \n",
    "    val_loss /= len(test_loader)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Early stopping logic\n",
    "    if val_loss < best_loss:\n",
    "        # If validation loss improves, save the model and reset the counter\n",
    "        best_loss = val_loss\n",
    "        counter = 0\n",
    "        best_model = model.state_dict()\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        counter += 1\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if counter >= patience:\n",
    "        print(f\"Early stopping at epoch {epoch+1}\")\n",
    "        break\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if (epoch + 1) % 10 == 0:\n",
    "        print(f\"Epoch {epoch+1}, Train Loss: {train_loss:.4f}, Val Loss: {val_loss:.4f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "model.load_state_dict(best_model)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Model Evaluation**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We evaluate the performance of the LSTM model using the same metrics as before (RMSE, MAE, R²) to compare it with the previous models. Before calculating the metrics, we inverse transform the predicted and actual values to get them back to their original scale."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "model.eval()\n",
    "y_pred_scaled = []\n",
    "\n",
    "# Inference loop to get predictions on the test set\n",
    "with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "    for X_batch, _ in test_loader:\n",
    "        preds = model(X_batch).squeeze().numpy()\n",
    "        y_pred_scaled.extend(preds)\n",
    "\n",
    "y_pred_scaled = np.array(y_pred_scaled)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Inverse-transform predictions per country\n",
    "y_pred_real = []\n",
    "y_test_real = []\n",
    "\n",
    "for i, country in enumerate(country_test):\n",
    "    scaler = scalers[country]\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Create dummy array for inverse_transform\n",
    "    dummy = np.zeros((1, X_test.shape[2]))  # number of features = X.shape[2]\n",
    "\n",
    "    # Set the target variable to the predicted value\n",
    "    dummy[0, target_index] = y_pred_scaled[i]\n",
    "    y_pred_real.append(scaler.inverse_transform(dummy)[0, target_index])\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Set the target variable to the actual value \n",
    "    dummy[0, target_index] = y_test[i].item()\n",
    "    y_test_real.append(scaler.inverse_transform(dummy)[0, target_index])\n",
    "\n",
    "y_pred_real = np.array(y_pred_real)\n",
    "y_test_real = np.array(y_test_real)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Evaluate the model's performance in the original temperature scale\n",
    "rmse = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_test_real, y_pred_real))\n",
    "mae = mean_absolute_error(y_test_real, y_pred_real)\n",
    "r2 = r2_score(y_test_real, y_pred_real)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Test RMSE:\", rmse)\n",
    "print(\"Test MAE:\", mae)\n",
    "print(\"Test R2:\", r2)"
   ]
